#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21868" :question/problem "$x^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0$\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4$. What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW^{′} &= -\\tan (x)W \\\\\n\\frac{dW}{W} &= -\\tan (x)dx \\\\\n∫\\frac{dW}{W} &= ∫ - \\tan (x)dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid W \\mid &= \\ln (\\cos x) + K \\\\\n\\mid W \\mid &= e^{K}\\cos x \\\\\nW &= C\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(0) &= C\\cos (0) \\\\\n4 &= C \\cdot 1 \\\\\nC &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute\n\n$$\nW\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(\\frac{π}{3}) &= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216955 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/id #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" :question/math-academy-id "e-21870" :question/problem "$y''+4y'+Q(x)y=0$\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3$. Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}$." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=4\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW^{′} &= -4W \\\\\n\\frac{dW}{W} &= -4dx \\\\\n∫\\frac{dW}{W} &= ∫ - 4dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid W \\mid &= -4x + K \\\\\n\\mid W \\mid &= e^{K}e^{-4x} \\\\\nW &= C e - 4 x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(2) &= Ce^{-4 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n- 3 &= Ce^{-8} \\\\\nC &= -3e^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that\n\n$$\nW(x) \\ne 0\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are $R$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1421ed9-8eac-58d0-9737-f173a3cc9186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216626} {:answer/id #uuid "f59ca442-68a0-5f1e-84eb-539db5ee393d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216627} {:answer/id #uuid "107bd02b-27ad-5ea4-a076-9b6f06cf6efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− xW(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216628} {:answer/id #uuid "357b3320-1e4f-5e24-88b0-fa310da7de21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sin (x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216629} {:answer/id #uuid "60bebb95-c94e-5bbf-a1bf-ec34b48e2b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\sin (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216630}] :answer-field/id #uuid "17b763af-91e4-597f-acba-401fdd402194" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216625}] :question/id #uuid "f2c11932-8453-50ce-aa58-370fc8f388e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342709" :question/problem "$y^{″} - x^{2}y^{′} + \\sin (x)y = 0$\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n$W^{′}(x) =$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217760 :knowledge-point/_